        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> To Become a Doctor, First I Will Need To...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: Communication: Facilitate a discussion on team roles in group work. Concept: Aid in interpreting differences in communication.</p>
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> 
            <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can facilitate a discussion on team roles for a career project, using gestures and visuals (e.g., pointing to each role on a poster and asking the peers about their ideas).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can aid in interpreting differences in communication styles by using both English and the native language, if needed, to explain career roles (e.g., using the term for "lawyer" in both languages while pointing to an image of a lawyer).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can clarify different job roles by miming common tasks (e.g., miming "helping" for a doctor and "teaching" for a teacher).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Warm-up:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays flashcards or images of various career roles (e.g., doctor, lawyer, teacher, engineer, chef). Students work in pairs to quickly match the image with the English word for the career. Then, the teacher asks, "What do these people want to achieve in their careers?" to connect to prior knowledge and elicit future tense plans. Students briefly share their ideas using "They want to be..." or "They will need to..." sentences.</p>
                <b>Modeling:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher models how to explain a career role and clarify potential misunderstandings. For example, pointing to a "doctor" image, the teacher says: "A doctor helps people who are sick. They need to study medicine. I would like to help people like a doctor. What does a doctor do?" (Teacher mimes helping). Then, pointing to a "lawyer" image: "A lawyer helps people with legal problems. Sometimes people confuse 'lawyer' with 'judge'. A lawyer represents people. A judge makes decisions. Do you understand the difference between a lawyer and a judge? I can explain it in another way." The teacher also models pronunciation of modals: should, would, will (/ʊ/ and /ɪ/) when expressing intentions or suggestions.</p>
                <b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone says, 'I want to be a professional,' what does 'professional' mean?" (Expected: Someone skilled in their job / Has a career.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I would like to train to be a teacher,' what am I going to do next?" (Expected: Go to university / Prepare to teach.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Careers".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Material:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher presents a simple example of a "Career Exploration Website Board" (pre-prepared or drawn on the board/poster). This board shows different career paths (e.g., 'Doctor's Path', 'Lawyer's Path') with simple diagrams, keywords, and connections. It includes example sentences with target grammar: "I want to be a doctor. I will need to go to university. I should complete an internship." It also shows sections for "Team Roles" within a group project and simple explanations, e.g., "Leader: helps the team organize," "Researcher: finds information."</p>
                <b>Communicative Competence:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will use language to express future intentions and plans, ask about career goals, and provide suggestions related to educational and career planning, especially when presenting or discussing a project.</p>
                <b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is this website board project about?" (Expected: It's about different future jobs and what we need to do to get them.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher points to a career path on the board and asks, "What qualifications will I need for this career?" or "What is a 'plan' for this job?" Students respond using vocabulary such as 'internship', 'study', 'train'.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Grammar: Wh-questions for career goals, future tense for plans (e.g., "I will need to..."), Modals for intention and suggestions (e.g., "I would like to..."), Want to + infinitive verb (e.g., "I want to be a doctor."). Vocabulary: doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, internship, qualifications, plan, practice. Pronunciation of modals: should, would, will (/ʊ/ and /ɪ/). Phonemic awareness: Minimal pairs (/ʃ/ vs /tʃ/, /dʒ/ vs /tʃ/).</p>
                <b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Clarifying Roles":</b> Students receive a handout with incomplete sentences about career roles and team tasks. They fill in the blanks using target vocabulary (e.g., "A ___ helps people in court. He is a ___." options: doctor/lawyer). They also complete sentences with mediating phrases like, "This means..." or "In other words..." Example: "To achieve success, I will need to ___. This means I must work hard." (practice/train). Focus on pronouncing modals like "should" and "would" clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice – "Explain and Interpret":</b> In pairs, students take turns explaining one specific career role or a task related to the "Career Exploration Website Board" project. One student says: "I want to be a teacher. I would like to teach children. I will need to get my qualifications." The partner must interpret by miming the action (e.g., teaching) or rephrasing: "So, you want to train to help students?" They use Wh-questions like, "What do you mean by 'qualifications'?" The explaining student clarifies, using gestures or even a brief native language term if needed, as per the learning outcome.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Project Role Discussion":</b> Students discuss potential team roles for their "Career Exploration Website Board" project. They assign roles (e.g., 'Designer', 'Content Creator', 'Presenter') and explain what each role entails. "I want to be the Designer. I will draw the web pages. What do you want to be? Perhaps you could be the Content Creator? That means you will write the descriptions. Do you agree?" They actively listen and clarify any confusion about roles or tasks. Teacher circulates, monitoring pronunciation and use of mediating language.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Careers".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Facilitated Discussion & Project Explanation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of 3-4 to present their "Career Exploration Website Board" project ideas. Each student takes a turn facilitating a discussion about a specific career path on their board or a team role. They explain their chosen career (e.g., "I want to be a lawyer. I will need to go to law school and then practice.") and the steps involved. During the explanation, they must actively use mediation strategies:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Using gestures and visuals (pointing to parts of their board) to clarify concepts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Miming common tasks associated with the career (e.g., a doctor helping a patient, a teacher writing on a board).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asking peers if they understand ("Do you know what 'internship' means?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responding to clarification questions from peers ("What do you mean by 'qualifications'?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If a peer struggles with a specific term, the student should attempt to explain it in simpler English, use an example, or provide the native language equivalent if necessary (e.g., "A 'lawyer' is also called 'abogado' in Spanish").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The other group members act as the audience, asking Wh-questions about career goals and plans (e.g., "What will you need to do to achieve your goals?").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">During the group presentations of their "Career Exploration Website Boards," the teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' mediation skills. Each student is assessed on their ability to explain their career plans and facilitate understanding among their peers.</p>
                <b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of modals (should, would, will).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, internship, qualifications, plan, practice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining career roles and future plans ("I want to be...", "I will need to...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of gestures and visuals to aid understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to clarification questions and aid in interpreting differences in communication (e.g., rephrasing, miming, or using L1 if necessary).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation in group activities, providing suggestions and asking questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing you explained clearly to your group about your career project today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you help a friend understand a difficult word or idea during our discussion?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did you face when trying to explain your ideas or career roles to your classmates, and how did you overcome them?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can your ability to explain and clarify information help you in future school projects or even in your future career?"</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of mediating information about future careers and project roles worked particularly well in this lesson? Were students confident in using gestures or rephrasing?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled to clarify or interpret information? Perhaps more explicit modeling of using L1 for specific terms or more structured scenarios for clarification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did the 21st Century Skills Project (Career Exploration Website Board) integrate with the mediation focus? Did it provide a natural context for students to practice facilitating discussions and clarifying information?</li>
                </ul>
                <b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This lesson concludes the "Future Careers" theme. Students have practiced mediating information about their career goals and plans, which is a crucial skill for collaborative work and effective communication in any future endeavor.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>